<Base
  title="Contact"
  description="Contact DaoismHub — how to report a correction, ask a sourcing question, request permission, make a privacy request or propose a collaboration, and what to expect in reply."
>
  <h1>Contact</h1>
  <p class="lede">Version 2.1 &middot; Last updated: October 23, 2026 &middot; Applies to daoismhub.com and data.daoismhub.com</p>

  <h2>Email</h2>
  <p>
    <strong><a href="mailto:martinwang2024@gmail.com">martinwang2024@gmail.com</a></strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the only contact channel for the site. There is no contact form, no phone line and
    no chat — deliberately, because the site collects no data and runs no accounts. Email is
    read by the editor.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you write</h2>
  <p>
    A few things are worth checking first, because they often save a round trip:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Search the site.</strong> Full-text <a href="/search/">search</a> covers the
      main site, and the Data Registry has its own in-browser search over the structured
      records. Many questions are already answered on a page you can find this way.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check the questions and problems collections.</strong> The
      <a href="/experiences/questions/">questions</a> and <a href="/problems/">problems</a>
      pages answer the things people most often ask — from posture to adverse experiences to
      why practice stalls.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Look at the policy pages first for policy matters.</strong> Reuse and copyright
      are covered in the <a href="/terms-of-service/">Terms of Service</a>; data handling in
      the <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> and <a href="/cookies/">Cookie Policy</a>;
      the limits of the content in the <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Write in English or Chinese.</strong> The site is published in English, but
      correspondence in Chinese is read and answered too.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to write about</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Corrections.</strong> A factual error, a mistranslation, a wrong attribution, a
      broken citation or a dead link. Please include the page URL and the exact sentence or
      passage, and — where you can — the source that settles it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sources.</strong> A primary text, edition or study we should cite, especially
      where it changes or contradicts what a page currently says.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Permissions.</strong> Syndication, translation, print, teaching use or any reuse
      beyond the permitted use set out in the
      <a href="/terms-of-service/">Terms of Service</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Privacy requests.</strong> Access, correction, erasure, objection or any other
      right described in the <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Collaboration.</strong> Research, translation or editorial partnerships on
      Chinese contemplative traditions. Please say what you propose and what you would bring.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Security.</strong> A vulnerability in either site. Please report it privately and
      do not test it against data that is not yours.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections — how they are handled</h2>
  <p>
    Corrections are part of how the site is maintained, and they are prioritised over other
    correspondence. When you report a factual or translation error:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>we check the claim against the source you point to, and against the source the page already cites;</li>
    <li>where a correction is substantiated, it is made and dated, and the page's update date changes;</li>
    <li>where a correction changes the substance of a page, the change is noted on the page rather than made silently;</li>
    <li>we may reply to confirm the outcome, or to ask for the source that settles a point where the report is ambiguous.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A report that simply disagrees with an interpretation, without a source, may not lead to a
    change — but it is still read, and where it identifies a genuine ambiguity the page will
    note that ambiguity if it does not already.
  </p>

  <h2>Permissions — common questions</h2>
  <p>
    The formal rules are in the <a href="/terms-of-service/">Terms of Service</a>. In
    practice, the questions are usually one of these:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>"Can I quote a short passage?"</strong> Yes — for private study, teaching,
      research, review or commentary, with credit to DaoismHub and a link to the source page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Can I translate a whole page or section?"</strong> That is republication of
      substantial content and needs written permission. Ask first; reasonable scholarly and
      educational use is usually granted.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Can I use this in my course or book?"</strong> For short quotation, yes with
      attribution; for extended reproduction, please ask. Tell us what you want to reuse,
      where it will appear and in what form.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Can I use the datasets?"</strong> For research and non-commercial use, yes with
      attribution, citing the schema and field definitions alongside the data. Commercial
      reuse needs written permission.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Privacy requests</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> describes your rights — access, correction,
    erasure, restriction, objection, portability and withdrawal of consent — and notes that,
    because the sites hold almost no personal data, the answer is usually that we hold nothing
    about you beyond an email you sent. To make a request, email with "Privacy" in the subject
    line. We may ask for enough information to locate any relevant correspondence, and no more
    than that. Privacy requests are acknowledged within seven days and resolved within one
    month, as the policy states.
  </p>

  <h2>Security reports</h2>
  <p>
    If you find a vulnerability, report it privately to the email address above rather than
    exploiting it or disclosing it publicly. Please do not access, modify or exfiltrate data
    that is not yours while testing. We will acknowledge your report, investigate, and tell
    you what we have done. There is no bug-bounty programme, and none is implied; reports are
    received as a courtesy to readers, and good reports are credited where the reporter
    wishes it.
  </p>

  <h2>What we cannot help with</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Medical, psychological or crisis questions.</strong> We cannot advise on your
      health, your practice or a condition, and we do not answer individual medical questions.
      If you are in crisis or in danger, contact your local emergency services or a qualified
      professional — see the <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Teacher or lineage referrals.</strong> The site does not recommend, rank or
      vouch for teachers, centres or organisations.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Personal practice instruction.</strong> The site documents practices; it does
      not teach them individually or supervise anyone's practice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Advertising, sponsorship and paid placement.</strong> These are not offered. See
      <a href="/about/">About</a> for the site's independence policy.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to get a useful reply</h2>
  <p>
    Put the subject of your message in the subject line — "Correction", "Source",
    "Permission", "Privacy", "Security" or "Collaboration" — and include the page URL where
    relevant. For corrections, quoting the passage is more useful than describing it. For
    permission requests, say what you want to reuse, where it will appear and in what form.
  </p>

  <h2>What to expect</h2>
  <p>
    The site is maintained by one editor, alongside other work. Corrections and privacy
    requests are prioritised: privacy requests are acknowledged within seven days and resolved
    within one month, as described in the <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>. Corrections
    are checked and, where substantiated, made and dated as soon as they can be verified.
    Other messages — source suggestions, permission requests, collaboration enquiries — are
    usually answered within one to two weeks.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a message needs no reply — a note of thanks, a source pointer that has simply been
    noted — it may not receive one, but it is read. If you need a reply and have not had one
    within two weeks, a single short follow-up is fine; please do not send the same message
    repeatedly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Please do not send confidential material. Anything sent may be treated as non-confidential
    and, for corrections and sources, may be reflected in the published page. If your message
    concerns your own health or practice, see the <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> — we
    cannot answer individual medical or practice questions.
  </p>

  <h2>Response times at a glance</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Message type</th><th>Typical response</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Privacy request</td><td>Acknowledged within 7 days; resolved within 1 month</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Correction</td><td>Checked as soon as possible; verified changes are made and dated</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Permission request</td><td>Usually answered within 1–2 weeks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Source suggestion</td><td>Usually answered within 1–2 weeks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Security report</td><td>Acknowledged promptly; outcome reported after investigation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Collaboration enquiry</td><td>Usually answered within 1–2 weeks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Note of thanks / a source pointer</td><td>May not receive a reply, but is read</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    These are targets, not guarantees: the site is run by a single editor alongside other
    work. If something is urgent on your side, say so in the subject line and in the first
    sentence, and it will be read with that in mind.
  </p>

  <h2>Editor</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ding.W</strong> — see the <a href="/about/">about page</a> for background, how
    content is made, and the site's editorial standards and independence policy. The editor
    is responsible for everything published here, and the <a href="/compliance/">Compliance</a>
    statement documents the evidence discipline the pages are held to.
  </p>
</Base>
